<h2><!--n-->. Transfer delegations' table-verify and expire catches keep logging when the thrown value is unreadable (KS-1868, transfer delegationService.ts)</h2>
<table class="pmatrix"><tr><td class="pm-topic" rowspan="2"><strong>What this changes.</strong></td><td> In <code>services/transfer/src/services/delegationService.ts</code>, the catch of <code>verifyDbReady</code> (the last step of the service's boot hook, <code>runDbBootTasks</code>) and the catch of <code>processExpiredDelegations</code> (called by the admin route <code>POST /api/delegations/admin/process-expired</code>) passed <code>err?.message</code> straight into <code>console.warn</code> / <code>console.error</code>. If the value is a revoked Proxy or an Error whose <code>message</code> getter throws, that read threw inside the catch: <code>verifyDbReady</code> rejected, and so did <code>processExpiredDelegations</code> instead of returning <code>0</code>. Each now reads the message inside a try (<code>String(message)</code>, or the constant <code>unreadable thrown value</code>). A hostile value therefore takes the outcome an ordinary Error already takes: the boot hook completes after a warning, and the admin route answers 200 with <code>expiredCount: 0</code> (a 500 before) although the UPDATE failed, as it does today for an ordinary Error.
    </td></tr><tr><td>Six new cells in one new test file, <code>ks1868-delegation-service-db-catches-unreadable-throw.test.ts</code> (109 lines): <code>control ks1868 C1</code> and <code>C2</code>, <code>RED ks1868 R1</code> to <code>R4</code>. The product bytes are <code>services/transfer/src/services/delegationService.ts</code> (+16/-2). A scratch recorder (not committed) called both functions with only the database module replaced (its <code>query</code> rejects with the value): 8 value kinds by 2 functions, base then head. At base both rejected for a revoked Proxy and a throwing getter (2 of 8 kinds each); at head 0 of 8 and every hostile row equals the ordinary-Error row.
    </td></tr></table>
<h3><!--n-->.1 What proves it</h3>
<table class="pmatrix"><tr><td class="pm-topic" rowspan="2"><strong>Red without the change, green with it, and no other cell moves.</strong></td><td> <strong>Red first, by assertion, develop <code>7ad0dcc14e5c</code>:</strong> with only the test hunk applied, 4 of 6 cells failed (R1 to R4, every failure an <code>AssertionError</code>, no timeout) and the 2 controls passed. With the product hunks: 6 of 6 pass.
    </td></tr><tr><td><strong>Whole transfer suite, before then after:</strong> 11 files / 139 cells then 12 / 145, each rc 0 with <code>--maxWorkers=2</code>. The default-worker runs at a load average of about 12 failed at the base and at the head alike, in the existing <code>transfer.test.ts</code> (a 10 s <code>beforeAll</code> timeout, so 28 cells did not run) and one existing KS-1868 cell (a 10 s test timeout); they are not counted. <code>tsc -p</code> on the service tsconfig: 0 errors, and 0 with the tests included, at base and head. eslint on the two changed files: no findings.
    </td></tr></table>
<h3><!--n-->.2 What it does not cover</h3>
<table class="pmatrix"><tr><td class="pm-topic" rowspan="1"><strong>Not measured here.</strong></td><td> A real Postgres or <code>pg</code> pool, a boot on a box, the admin route over HTTP (the caller is read, not run), whether a real thrown value of this kind occurs, the other KS-1868 sites, whole-suite CI, the four platform suites and Node 24. KS-1868 stays open.
    </td></tr></table>
